#{[17592186259539 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{7{n}^{3}}{{e}^{n/10}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{7{n}^{3}}{{e}^{n/10}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges and its limit is $0.$"] [17592186259540 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{log}_{2}⁡n}-1)=-1+\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{log}_{2}⁡n}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{log}_{2}⁡n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{log}_{2}⁡n}-1)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges."] [17592186259541 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{\\sqrt{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges,  and its limit is $0.$"] [17592186259542 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(100n)}{n\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(100n)}{n\\sqrt{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges,  and its limit is $0.$"] [17592186259543 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5n)}{{n}^{5}}-1)=-1+\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5n)}{{n}^{5}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5n)}{{n}^{5}}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=-1+\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5n)}{{n}^{5}}=-1+0=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges and its limit is $-1.$"] [17592186259544 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit, we calculate\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{log}_{7}⁡n}{{e}^{3n}}-1)=-1+\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{7}⁡n}{{e}^{3n}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{7}⁡n}{{e}^{3n}}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=-1+\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{7}⁡n}{{e}^{3n}}=-1+0=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges and its limit is $-1.$"] [17592186259545 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit, we calculate\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡n}{{e}^{n/10}}-1)=-1+\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡n}{{e}^{n/10}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡n}{{e}^{n/10}}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=-1+\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡n}{{e}^{n/10}}=-1+0=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges and its limit is $-1.$"] [17592186259546 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit, we calculate\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{n/10}}{{log}_{5}⁡n}-1)=-1+\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n/10}}{{log}_{5}⁡n}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n/10}}{{log}_{5}⁡n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{n/10}}{{log}_{5}⁡n}-1)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges."] [17592186262677 :que